We have derived improved differentiability properties for solutions to a non-autonomous obstacle problem, formulated as
  \begin{equation*}
    \min \left\{ \int_{\Omega} f\left(x, \nabla u, \nabla^2 u\right) dx, \int_{\Omega} \omega(x) |\u - \obst|^p dx \right\},
  \end{equation*}
  where the function $f$ strictly decreases with the Hessian gradient variable, and grows and behaves partially with respect to the first-order gradient variable within a Nirenberg type range, and we have also considered the case of p-growth and p-degeneracy. Furthermore, if $f$ also satisfies a small perturbation with respect to the gradient variable, we have achieved a small growth result in relation to p. Furthermore, we have considered applications to a class of quasi-convex functionals and p-Laplacian type problems.
